348
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:50,200
 So this is just kind of a note

349
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:55,180
 for non-Latin character sets.

350
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:57,600
 You know, all programming languages from the '60s on

351
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:01,980
 tended to work in what we call the Latin character set,

352
00:16:01,980 --> 00:16:05,520
 which is the United States and England and Europe

353
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:08,820
 and lots of places use this ABC character set

354
00:16:08,820 --> 00:16:10,680
 and the special characters.

355
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:15,520
 But it's really common to want to use different characters.

356
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:18,680
 And so if you're going from Python 2 to Python 3,

357
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:20,220
 and we'll talk about this a little later

358
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:22,600
 when it matters more.

359
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:26,780
 Luckily we're in Python 3, and so one of the big things

360
00:16:26,780 --> 00:16:31,120
 about Python 3 is that all the internal strings are Unicode.

361
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:35,420
 In Python 2, there was sort of some confusion

362
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:36,940
 as you went between strings.

363
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:38,520
 And this is just a little bit of code.

364
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:40,940
 And so I'm putting a in here.

365
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:46,100
 You know, some Asian characters, this is Korean actually,

366
00:16:46,100 --> 00:16:48,840
 Asian characters into X, and then I say

367
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:52,980
 what kind of a thing this is, and that is a string.

368
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:55,040
 And then there's this Unicode.

369
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:57,280
 And this comes from Python 2.

370
00:16:57,280 --> 00:17:00,960
 If it's a Unicode operation, it's still a string.

371
00:17:00,960 --> 00:17:04,540
 Whereas in Python 2, if you put international characters

372
00:17:04,540 --> 00:17:06,580
 into X, then it was a string.

373
00:17:06,580 --> 00:17:08,980
 And then there was a separate kind of a constant

374
00:17:08,980 --> 00:17:11,780
 called a Unicode constant, and it was a different type.

375
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:15,060
 And there was ways that you had to mess

376
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:17,720
 with these Unicode variables as you did things

377
00:17:17,720 --> 00:17:20,820
 like read them from files and put them back into files

378
00:17:20,820 --> 00:17:21,860
 and did other things.

379
00:17:21,860 --> 00:17:24,360
 So it was much more difficult

380
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:29,620
 in Python 2, but we're doing in Python 3.

381
00:17:29,620 --> 00:17:33,660
 And in Python 3, it natively understands

382
00:17:33,660 --> 00:17:37,340
 non-Latin character sets, international Asian character sets,

383
00:17:37,340 --> 00:17:39,180
 Spanish, French character sets.

384
00:17:39,180 --> 00:17:41,460
 And so this is a good thing for Python 3,

385
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:44,700
 and this is one of the real benefits of using Python 3.

